\keepn\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel0\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f1\fs28\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\b Guidelines for Choosing Good Passwords
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f1\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
\par 
\par Passwords are secrets that should never be shared, not even with your help desk or your best friend. (Would you share your toothbrush? your underwear?) They tell the computer that you really are the person you claim to be. If someone else uses your passwo
rd, you will be responsible for his or her actions. A password should always contain a mix of }{\i both letters and numbers}{. Real words should never be used since password \'93crackers\'94 can easily compare your password to a dictionary.
\par }\pard\plain \s16\ql \li0\ri0\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f1\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f1\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\b DO:
\par }{Do always use a mix of both numbers and letters. If your computer system permits it, you should also include special characters such as %.
\par Do make passwords at least six [or seven or eight] alphanumeric characters long.
\par Do develop your own scheme (and keep it secret!) so that you will remember your passwords without having to write them down or call your help desk.
\par }\pard\plain \s15\ql \li720\ri0\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 \f1\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
For example, if you love music, use the first letters of a line in a song or the name of a symphony; then insert a number that\rquote s meaningful to you. Then \'93America The Beautiful\'94 can become A7T6B5. If you\rquote 
re a reader, use book titles.  Or movie buffs can use film titles. 
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f1\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
\par }{\b DON\rquote T:
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {Don\rquote t use real words, even if they are in a foreign language. 
\par Don\rquote t repeat your user ID (username, login ID, etc.) as your password.
\par Don\rquote t use names of people, pets, fictional or fantasy characters, car models, sports teams, etc.
\par Don\rquote t use your license number or other similar certificate numbers even though they may combine numbers and letters; they aren\rquote t secret.
\par Don\rquote t write passwords down. (Exception: If you need to, write it down when you\rquote 
re making it up. The visual reinforcement helps you remember it. But then destroy the piece of paper.) If you absolutely have to write it down (for example, you use it infrequently), then be sure it\rquote s in a very secure place and, if found, can
\rquote t be associated with a particular computer system. 
\par Don\rquote t ever write your password on a security authentication \'93token\'94 or \'93smart card\'94 (or your ATM card) if you are issued one. This defeats the purpose of such a device.
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {
\par Keep these guidelines in mind whenever you\rquote re asked to choose a password\emdash at work, at your bank, or on the Internet.
\par }}